  <section id="laundry-basics" class="content-section">
    <h2>1. Laundry Fundamentals</h2>

    <p>
      Good laundry care begins before clothing enters the washing
      machine. Preparing garments correctly helps prevent colour
      transfer, fabric damage, shrinkage, and unnecessary wear.
    </p>

    <h3>Sorting Laundry</h3>
    <p>
      Separate laundry according to colour, fabric type, soil level,
      and washing requirements. A practical sorting system includes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Whites and light-coloured garments.</li>
      <li>Dark clothing, including black, navy, and charcoal.</li>
      <li>Bright and deeply coloured garments.</li>
      <li>Delicate fabrics such as silk, lace, and fine knits.</li>
      <li>Towels and household linens.</li>
      <li>Heavily soiled clothing and work garments.</li>
      <li>Sportswear and technical performance fabrics.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Preparing Garments</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Read garment-care labels before washing.</li>
      <li>Empty pockets and remove loose objects.</li>
      <li>Close zippers and fasten hooks to prevent snagging.</li>
      <li>Turn dark garments and printed clothing inside out.</li>
      <li>Separate items with delicate buttons, embroidery, or embellishments.</li>
      <li>Use mesh laundry bags for suitable delicate garments.</li>
      <li>Check stains and pretreat them when appropriate.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Loading the Washing Machine</h3>
    <p>
      Avoid overloading the machine. Clothing needs room to move
      freely so that water and detergent can circulate properly.
      An overloaded washer may leave detergent residue, produce
      uneven cleaning, and increase fabric friction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="detergent-types" class="content-section">
    <h2>2. Understanding Different Types of Laundry Detergents</h2>

    <p>
      Laundry detergents are formulated to remove soil, body oils,
      food residues, and everyday stains. They contain combinations
      of surfactants, builders, enzymes, water conditioners,
      fragrances, and other ingredients.
    </p>

    <h3>Powder Laundry Detergent</h3>
    <p>
      Powder detergent is commonly used for everyday laundry and
      can be particularly useful for certain general soil and
      outdoor dirt. Some powder formulations contain oxygen-based
      bleaching ingredients that help brighten suitable washable
      fabrics.
    </p>
    <p>
      Powder should be measured according to the manufacturer's
      instructions and the water conditions. It may not dissolve
      as readily in very cold water, particularly when overdosed
      or used in an overloaded machine.
    </p>

    <h3>Liquid Laundry Detergent</h3>
    <p>
      Liquid detergents dissolve readily in water and are convenient
      for many everyday washing requirements. They can be useful
      for pretreating certain greasy or oily stains when the product
      label permits direct application.
    </p>
    <p>
      Measure carefully. Using too much liquid detergent can leave
      residue on clothing and contribute to buildup inside the
      washing machine.
    </p>

    <h3>Laundry Detergent Pods and Capsules</h3>
    <p>
      Pods and capsules contain premeasured detergent, offering
      convenience and reducing the need to measure each wash.
      They are generally placed directly into the empty washing
      drum before adding laundry, according to product directions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not cut, puncture, or dissolve capsules separately unless
      the manufacturer specifically instructs you to do so.
      Keep them securely stored away from children and pets.
    </p>

    <h3>High-Efficiency (HE) Detergent</h3>
    <p>
      High-efficiency washing machines use less water than many
      traditional machines. HE detergents are formulated to produce
      controlled sudsing and work effectively in these conditions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use detergent marked HE when required by your washing machine.
      Excessive suds can interfere with washing and rinsing performance.
    </p>

    <h3>Concentrated Detergents</h3>
    <p>
      Concentrated detergents contain less water or filler relative
      to their cleaning ingredients. They require smaller measured
      quantities than many conventional formulations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Always use the dosage recommended on the packaging rather
      than estimating by eye.
    </p>

    <h3>Enzyme-Based Detergents</h3>
    <p>
      Enzymes help break down particular types of organic stains.
      Different enzymes perform different functions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Proteases help break down protein-based stains such as blood and egg.</li>
      <li>Amylases help break down starch-based food stains.</li>
      <li>Lipases help break down certain fats and oils.</li>
      <li>Cellulases can help maintain the appearance of some cotton fabrics.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Enzyme detergents can be effective for everyday family laundry.
      However, check fabric-care instructions before using them on
      delicate or specialty garments. Some delicate fabrics and
      specialty finishes require different products.
    </p>

    <h3>Detergents for Delicate Fabrics</h3>
    <p>
      Delicate detergents are formulated for garments requiring
      gentler treatment. They are commonly used for suitable silk,
      wool, cashmere, lace, and other fine fabrics.
    </p>
    <p>
      Look for products specifically labelled as suitable for
      the fibre being washed. A wool-specific detergent is not
      automatically appropriate for every silk garment, and
      care labels should always take priority.
    </p>

    <h3>Wool and Cashmere Wash</h3>
    <p>
      Wool and cashmere benefit from products formulated for
      animal fibres. These detergents are generally designed
      to clean gently while helping preserve the natural feel
      of the fibres.
    </p>
    <p>
      Avoid ordinary harsh detergents and products containing
      unsuitable bleaching ingredients when caring for fine wool.
    </p>

    <h3>Colour-Care Detergents</h3>
    <p>
      Colour-care detergents are designed to clean coloured
      clothing while helping reduce colour fading. They are
      useful for dark garments, bright colours, and many
      everyday coloured fabrics.
    </p>

    <h3>Fragrance-Free and Sensitive-Skin Detergents</h3>
    <p>
      Fragrance-free detergents are useful for people who prefer
      to avoid added fragrance. Some formulations are also
      designed to minimize certain ingredients that may irritate
      sensitive skin.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fragrance-free does not necessarily mean hypoallergenic.
      Check the complete ingredient information and choose a
      product appropriate for your household.
    </p>

    <h3>Specialty Laundry Detergents</h3>
    <p>
      Specialty detergents are available for particular laundry
      requirements, including baby clothing, sportswear, dark
      garments, outdoor clothing, and heavily soiled workwear.
      Select products according to their intended use and
      the garment manufacturer's instructions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="fabric-softeners" class="content-section">
    <h2>3. Fabric Softeners & Fabric Conditioners</h2>

    <p>
      Fabric softeners and conditioners are used to improve the
      feel of many washable fabrics, reduce static electricity,
      and provide fragrance. They are generally added during
      the rinse stage rather than the main wash.
    </p>

    <h3>Liquid Fabric Softener</h3>
    <p>
      Liquid fabric softeners are added to the designated
      dispenser in the washing machine. They coat fibres with
      conditioning ingredients that help reduce friction and
      create a softer feel.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the recommended amount. Excessive softener can build
      up on fabrics and reduce the performance of certain
      textiles.
    </p>

    <h3>Concentrated Fabric Conditioner</h3>
    <p>
      Concentrated conditioners are designed to deliver
      conditioning benefits in smaller quantities. Follow
      the measuring instructions carefully, as using too
      much can leave a coating on garments.
    </p>

    <h3>Fragranced Fabric Softeners</h3>
    <p>
      Fragranced softeners leave clothing with a noticeable
      scent. Fragrance intensity varies between formulations.
      For people sensitive to fragrance, an unscented option
      may be preferable.
    </p>

    <h3>Dryer Sheets</h3>
    <p>
      Dryer sheets are placed in the tumble dryer and can
      reduce static and soften many fabrics. Some also
      provide fragrance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check the garment and dryer instructions before use.
      Dryer sheets are not appropriate for every fabric or
      technical textile.
    </p>

    <h3>Wool Dryer Balls</h3>
    <p>
      Wool dryer balls are reusable dryer accessories that
      help separate items as they tumble. They can encourage
      airflow and may help reduce drying time in some loads.
      They do not provide the same conditioning treatment
      as conventional fabric softeners.
    </p>

    <h3>When Not to Use Fabric Softener</h3>
    <p>
      Fabric softener is not suitable for every textile.
      In particular, avoid it on:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Microfibre cleaning cloths, where absorbency can be reduced.</li>
      <li>Many towels, because coatings can reduce water absorption.</li>
      <li>Technical sportswear designed to wick moisture.</li>
      <li>Some flame-resistant garments, unless the manufacturer permits it.</li>
      <li>Some waterproof and water-repellent performance fabrics.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Always follow the garment manufacturer's recommendations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="disinfectants" class="content-section">
    <h2>4. Laundry Disinfectants, Sanitizers & Bleaching Products</h2>

    <p>
      Cleaning, sanitizing, and disinfecting are not identical
      processes. Ordinary laundry detergent removes soil and
      many microorganisms through washing and rinsing, but it
      should not automatically be considered a disinfectant.
    </p>

    <h3>Laundry Sanitizers</h3>
    <p>
      Laundry sanitizers are specialty products designed to
      reduce specified microorganisms on suitable fabrics
      when used according to their labelled directions.
      Some are formulated for use in particular rinse cycles
      or washing temperatures.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check the product label for the fabrics, machine settings,
      contact times, and intended uses. In Canada, look for
      appropriate regulatory authorization and follow the
      directions on the product.
    </p>

    <h3>Chlorine Bleach</h3>
    <p>
      Chlorine bleach is a powerful bleaching and disinfecting
      product for suitable washable materials. It can whiten
      certain fabrics and help remove some stains.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not appropriate for every garment. It can damage
      coloured fabrics, silk, wool, elastane, and other
      sensitive fibres.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use only where the garment label and product instructions
      permit. Never mix chlorine bleach with ammonia, acids,
      vinegar, or other cleaning products.
    </p>

    <h3>Oxygen-Based Bleach</h3>
    <p>
      Oxygen bleach products commonly contain ingredients such
      as sodium percarbonate. They release oxygen in water
      and can help brighten suitable washable fabrics and
      treat certain stains.
    </p>
    <p>
      Oxygen bleach is different from chlorine bleach and
      should not automatically be considered a disinfectant.
      Follow product directions and check fabric compatibility.
    </p>

    <h3>Colour-Safe Bleaching Products</h3>
    <p>
      Some oxygen-based products are marketed for use on
      colourfast garments. They can help address certain
      stains while being less aggressive than chlorine
      bleach on suitable materials.
    </p>
    <p>
      Test colourfastness where recommended and avoid assuming
      that every coloured or delicate fabric is compatible.
    </p>

    <h3>When to Consider Laundry Sanitizing</h3>
    <p>
      A suitable laundry sanitizer may be useful for certain
      household situations, such as washing appropriate
      washable items that require additional hygiene measures.
      Examples may include some sports clothing, towels,
      and household textiles.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose products according to the specific need and
      follow the label. Routine laundry does not necessarily
      require a separate disinfectant.
    </p>

    <h3>Important Chemical Safety</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Never mix bleach with ammonia, vinegar, acids, or other cleaners.</li>
      <li>Keep products in their original labelled containers.</li>
      <li>Use products only for their labelled purpose.</li>
      <li>Provide ventilation when using appropriate cleaning products.</li>
      <li>Wear protective equipment when the label recommends it.</li>
      <li>Store chemicals securely away from children and pets.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="washing-temperatures" class="content-section">
    <h2>5. Choosing the Correct Washing Temperature</h2>

    <p>
      Water temperature affects cleaning performance, colour
      retention, fabric shrinkage, and energy use. The correct
      temperature depends on the textile, detergent, level of
      soiling, and garment-care instructions.
    </p>

    <h3>Cold Water</h3>
    <p>
      Cold washing is useful for many everyday garments,
      dark colours, and delicate textiles. It can help reduce
      colour fading and shrinkage and is often more energy
      efficient.
    </p>
    <p>
      Modern detergents are commonly formulated to work in
      cold water. However, heavily soiled items and certain
      hygiene requirements may need a different approach.
    </p>

    <h3>Cool Water</h3>
    <p>
      Cool water is a practical choice for many mixed-colour
      loads and lightly soiled clothing. It can provide a
      balance between gentle fabric treatment and everyday
      cleaning.
    </p>

    <h3>Warm Water</h3>
    <p>
      Warm water may be appropriate for certain cotton garments,
      bedding, towels, and moderately soiled laundry when
      the care label permits it.
    </p>

    <h3>Hot Water</h3>
    <p>
      Hot water is appropriate only for suitable fabrics and
      when the garment and product instructions allow it.
      It can be useful for certain heavily soiled washable
      items, but may shrink fabrics, fade colours, or damage
      delicate fibres.
    </p>

    <h3>Temperature and Hygiene</h3>
    <p>
      Washing temperature alone does not guarantee disinfection.
      For a specific hygiene requirement, follow the
      instructions of an appropriately labelled laundry
      sanitizer or disinfectant and the washing machine
      manufacturer's recommendations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="silk-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>6. Caring for Silk & Delicate Fabrics</h2>

    <p>
      Silk is a natural protein fibre prized for its smooth
      texture, lustre, drape, and luxurious appearance.
      It is used in blouses, dresses, scarves, lingerie,
      ties, and fine bedding.
    </p>

    <h3>Understanding Silk</h3>
    <p>
      Silk can be sensitive to harsh detergents, strong
      alkaline products, excessive heat, prolonged soaking,
      and rough handling. Some silk garments are also
      vulnerable to water spotting or changes in appearance.
    </p>

    <h3>Washing Silk</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Check the care label first; some silk garments require dry cleaning.</li>
      <li>Use a detergent specifically suitable for washable silk.</li>
      <li>Use cool water and a gentle hand-washing or machine cycle only when permitted.</li>
      <li>Wash similar colours together and avoid overcrowding.</li>
      <li>Do not use chlorine bleach.</li>
      <li>Avoid harsh stain removers and vigorous rubbing.</li>
      <li>Do not wring or twist silk to remove water.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Drying Silk</h3>
    <p>
      After washing, gently press excess water out using a
      clean towel. Reshape the garment and dry it flat or
      according to the manufacturer's instructions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Avoid direct sunlight, high heat, and tumble drying
      unless the garment label specifically permits it.
    </p>

    <h3>Ironing Silk</h3>
    <p>
      Use a low-temperature silk setting where appropriate.
      Iron garments inside out and preferably while slightly
      damp or with a suitable pressing cloth. Avoid direct
      high heat, which can damage the fibres and affect
      the fabric's lustre.
    </p>

    <h3>Silk Scarves</h3>
    <p>
      Fine silk scarves deserve especially gentle treatment.
      Avoid snagging them on jewellery, rough surfaces,
      and sharp accessories. Store them clean, dry, and
      loosely folded.
    </p>

    <h3>Silk Storage</h3>
    <p>
      Store silk garments in a cool, dry, clean environment
      away from direct sunlight. Avoid hanging delicate
      bias-cut or heavily embellished pieces if their
      weight could distort their shape.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cashmere-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>7. Caring for Cashmere & Fine Wool</h2>

    <p>
      Cashmere is a fine natural fibre obtained from the
      undercoat of cashmere goats. It is valued for its
      softness, warmth, lightweight feel, and luxurious
      texture.
    </p>

    <h3>Why Cashmere Requires Special Care</h3>
    <p>
      Fine cashmere fibres can felt, shrink, stretch, or
      lose their shape when exposed to unsuitable washing
      conditions, agitation, or heat.
    </p>

    <h3>Washing Cashmere</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Read the garment label before deciding whether to wash or dry clean.</li>
      <li>Use a detergent specifically formulated for wool and cashmere.</li>
      <li>Use cool water and gentle handling when hand washing is permitted.</li>
      <li>Avoid vigorous rubbing, twisting, and wringing.</li>
      <li>Rinse gently if the detergent instructions require it.</li>
      <li>Never use chlorine bleach.</li>
      <li>Avoid ordinary fabric softeners unless the garment manufacturer permits them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Drying Cashmere</h3>
    <p>
      Gently remove excess water by pressing the garment
      between clean towels. Reshape it carefully and dry
      flat on a clean, dry surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hanging wet cashmere can cause stretching because
      the weight of the water pulls on the fibres. Avoid
      tumble drying and direct heat.
    </p>

    <h3>Removing Pilling</h3>
    <p>
      Cashmere can develop small fibre balls known as pills,
      especially in areas of friction such as under the arms
      and along the sides of a garment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use a suitable cashmere comb or fabric de-pilling tool
      gently. Do not pull pills off by hand, as this can
      damage the fibres.
    </p>

    <h3>Storing Cashmere</h3>
    <p>
      Store clean cashmere folded rather than hanging.
      Use breathable storage bags or containers and protect
      garments from moths. Avoid storing cashmere while
      damp or soiled.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cotton-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>8. Cotton & Fine Cotton Fabrics</h2>

    <p>
      Cotton is a natural plant fibre used in everyday
      clothing, shirts, bedding, towels, and household
      textiles. It is generally durable and versatile,
      although different weaves, finishes, and garment
      constructions require different care.
    </p>

    <h3>Everyday Cotton</h3>
    <p>
      T-shirts, casual shirts, cotton trousers, and many
      everyday garments can be machine washed according
      to their care labels. Separate colours and avoid
      excessive heat to help reduce shrinkage and fading.
    </p>

    <h3>Egyptian Cotton</h3>
    <p>
      Egyptian cotton is associated with long cotton fibres
      used to produce fine textiles. Its softness and
      durability depend on fibre quality, yarn construction,
      weave, and manufacturing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Wash Egyptian cotton bedding and garments according
      to their labels. Avoid excessive detergent and
      unnecessary high-temperature drying.
    </p>

    <h3>Percale Cotton</h3>
    <p>
      Percale is a closely woven fabric with a crisp,
      matte finish. It is commonly used in bedding.
      Wash according to the manufacturer's instructions
      and avoid excessive fabric conditioner if you want
      to maintain its crisp feel.
    </p>

    <h3>Cotton Sateen</h3>
    <p>
      Cotton sateen has a smoother, often lustrous surface
      created by its weave. Use appropriate washing
      temperatures and avoid harsh treatment that could
      damage the surface.
    </p>

    <h3>Preventing Cotton Shrinkage</h3>
    <p>
      Excessive heat during washing and drying can cause
      some cotton garments to shrink. Follow the care
      label, avoid unnecessary hot washing, and use
      moderate drying temperatures where permitted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="dress-shirt-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>9. Dress Shirts & Tailored Clothing</h2>

    <p>
      Dress shirts are often made from fine cotton, cotton
      blends, linen, or other woven fabrics. A well-cared-for
      shirt should retain its shape, crisp collar, clean
      cuffs, and smooth appearance.
    </p>

    <h3>Preparing Dress Shirts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Check the care label and fabric composition.</li>
      <li>Remove collar stays before washing, if removable.</li>
      <li>Unbutton the shirt, including cuffs.</li>
      <li>Check collars and cuffs for body oils, cosmetics, and dirt.</li>
      <li>Separate whites from coloured shirts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Washing Dress Shirts</h3>
    <p>
      Many cotton dress shirts can be machine washed using
      an appropriate gentle or normal cycle. Fine fabrics,
      embellished shirts, and shirts with special finishes
      may require more careful treatment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use a suitable detergent and avoid excessive amounts
      of fabric conditioner, which may affect the crisp
      feel of some shirt fabrics.
    </p>

    <h3>Collars & Cuffs</h3>
    <p>
      Collars and cuffs often collect perspiration, body
      oils, skin products, and everyday soil. Apply a
      suitable pre-treatment product according to its
      directions before washing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Work gently. Aggressive scrubbing can damage the
      fabric and wear down the collar edges.
    </p>

    <h3>Whitening White Dress Shirts</h3>
    <p>
      White shirts can gradually become dull from body
      oils, detergent residue, and repeated wear. Use
      a suitable detergent and, where permitted, an
      appropriate oxygen-based whitening product.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not assume chlorine bleach is suitable for every
      white shirt. It can weaken fibres, damage finishes,
      and cause yellowing in some circumstances.
    </p>

    <h3>Ironing Dress Shirts</h3>
    <p>
      For a neat finish, iron shirts while slightly damp
      or use steam where the fabric permits. Follow the
      iron's fabric settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      A practical ironing sequence is:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Collar: work from the points toward the centre.</li>
      <li>Cuffs: open and press carefully.</li>
      <li>Sleeves: align the seams and press smoothly.</li>
      <li>Shoulders: position the shirt over the narrow end of the ironing board.</li>
      <li>Front panels: work around buttons carefully.</li>
      <li>Back: finish with long, smooth strokes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Storing Dress Shirts</h3>
    <p>
      Hang shirts on appropriately sized hangers after
      they are completely dry. Fasten the top button
      where useful to help maintain the collar shape.
      Allow garments room to breathe in the closet.
    </p>

    <h3>Tailored Suits & Jackets</h3>
    <p>
      Suits, structured jackets, and tailored garments
      often contain internal canvassing, shoulder padding,
      linings, and specialty fabrics. These garments
      should generally be maintained according to their
      care labels, with professional cleaning where
      required.
    </p>
    <p>
      Between wears, air garments in a ventilated area
      and use suitable garment brushes to remove surface
      dust. Avoid unnecessary dry cleaning, which can
      contribute to fabric wear.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="linen-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>10. Linen & Natural Plant Fibres</h2>

    <p>
      Linen is made from flax fibres and is valued for
      its natural texture, breathability, and distinctive
      appearance. It is commonly used in summer clothing,
      table linens, bedding, and household textiles.
    </p>

    <h3>Washing Linen</h3>
    <p>
      Wash linen according to its care label using a
      suitable detergent. Avoid overcrowding the machine
      and use a cycle appropriate for the garment's
      construction and colour.
    </p>

    <h3>Drying Linen</h3>
    <p>
      Linen can become stiff or excessively wrinkled
      if dried too aggressively. Air drying or a
      suitable low-temperature tumble-dry setting
      can help preserve its appearance.
    </p>

    <h3>Ironing Linen</h3>
    <p>
      Linen is naturally prone to creasing. Ironing
      while slightly damp can make wrinkles easier
      to remove. Use the temperature recommended
      for the fabric and test an inconspicuous area
      where necessary.
    </p>

    <h3>Other Natural Plant Fibres</h3>
    <p>
      Hemp and other plant-based fibres may also be
      used in household textiles and clothing.
      Their washing requirements depend on the
      weave, dye, finishing, and garment construction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="wool-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>11. Wool & Other Animal Fibres</h2>

    <p>
      Natural animal fibres include wool, merino,
      lambswool, alpaca, mohair, and cashmere.
      They are valued for warmth, softness, and
      their natural insulating properties.
    </p>

    <h3>Merino Wool</h3>
    <p>
      Merino wool is known for its fine fibres and
      softness. Some merino garments are machine
      washable on a wool cycle, while others require
      hand washing or professional care.
    </p>

    <h3>Lambswool</h3>
    <p>
      Lambswool garments should be handled gently.
      Use an appropriate wool detergent and avoid
      agitation, high heat, and hanging wet garments.
    </p>

    <h3>Alpaca</h3>
    <p>
      Alpaca fibres are valued for their softness
      and warmth. Care requirements vary by garment,
      but gentle handling and flat drying are often
      important for preserving shape.
    </p>

    <h3>Mohair</h3>
    <p>
      Mohair has a distinctive lustre and may be
      used in sweaters, scarves, and other textiles.
      Follow the garment label carefully, as
      excessive friction can affect its appearance.
    </p>

    <h3>Preventing Moth Damage</h3>
    <p>
      Clean wool and animal-fibre garments before
      long-term storage. Store them in clean,
      dry, protective containers and inspect
      periodically for signs of insect damage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="synthetic-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>12. Synthetic & Performance Fabrics</h2>

    <p>
      Synthetic fibres are widely used in everyday
      clothing, sportswear, outerwear, and technical
      garments. Common examples include polyester,
      nylon, acrylic, and elastane.
    </p>

    <h3>Polyester</h3>
    <p>
      Polyester is commonly used in shirts, trousers,
      dresses, fleece, and household textiles.
      Wash according to the garment label and
      avoid excessive heat when drying or ironing.
    </p>

    <h3>Nylon</h3>
    <p>
      Nylon is used in hosiery, outerwear, sportswear,
      and other durable textiles. Use appropriate
      washing temperatures and avoid high dryer
      temperatures that could damage the fibres.
    </p>

    <h3>Elastane and Spandex</h3>
    <p>
      Elastane provides stretch and is often blended
      with other fibres. High heat and unsuitable
      chemicals can damage its elasticity.
      Use gentle washing and avoid excessive
      tumble-drying heat.
    </p>

    <h3>Acrylic</h3>
    <p>
      Acrylic is commonly used in knitwear and
      blankets. Follow the garment label and
      avoid excessive heat, which can distort
      the fabric.
    </p>

    <h3>Sportswear & Moisture-Wicking Fabrics</h3>
    <p>
      Performance garments are designed to manage
      moisture, temperature, and movement. Wash
      them according to manufacturer instructions,
      often using a suitable sportswear detergent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Avoid fabric softener when it can interfere
      with moisture-wicking performance. Allow
      garments to dry thoroughly before storing
      them.
    </p>

    <h3>Waterproof & Water-Repellent Clothing</h3>
    <p>
      Technical jackets and outdoor clothing may
      have specialist coatings or membranes.
      Use detergents specifically approved for
      the garment and follow the manufacturer's
      instructions for washing and restoring
      water repellency.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="denim-care" class="content-section">
    <h2>13. Denim & Everyday Clothing</h2>

    <p>
      Denim is a durable cotton-based fabric that
      may be blended with elastane or other fibres.
      Its colour and finish can change with repeated
      washing, heat, and friction.
    </p>

    <h3>Washing Jeans</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Wash dark denim inside out.</li>
      <li>Separate new or deeply dyed garments from light clothing.</li>
      <li>Use a suitable detergent and cool water where permitted.</li>
      <li>Avoid unnecessary washing when airing is sufficient.</li>
      <li>Do not overload the machine.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Drying Denim</h3>
    <p>
      Air drying can help preserve the colour and
      fit of many denim garments. Reshape the jeans
      while damp and hang or lay them according
      to the manufacturer's recommendations.
    </p>

    <h3>Everyday Clothing</h3>
    <p>
      T-shirts, casual trousers, blouses, and everyday
      garments benefit from sorting by colour and
      fabric type. Turn printed garments inside out
      and avoid excessive detergent, heat, and
      unnecessary friction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="towels-bedding" class="content-section">
    <h2>14. Towels & Bedding</h2>

    <h3>Bath Towels</h3>
    <p>
      Towels should be washed according to their
      care labels and allowed to dry completely
      between uses. Avoid excessive detergent and
      fabric softener, which can leave residue
      and reduce absorbency.
    </p>

    <h3>White Towels</h3>
    <p>
      Suitable white cotton towels may be washed
      with an appropriate detergent and, where
      permitted, a compatible oxygen-based
      whitening product.
    </p>

    <h3>Bed Sheets</h3>
    <p>
      Cotton, linen, bamboo-derived, and blended
      bed sheets can have different washing
      requirements. Follow the care label and
      avoid excessive heat that may cause
      shrinkage or premature wear.
    </p>

    <h3>Down & Feather Bedding</h3>
    <p>
      Down duvets and feather pillows require
      careful cleaning and thorough drying.
      Follow the manufacturer's instructions
      and use a suitable professional cleaning
      service when required.
    </p>

    <h3>Mattress Protectors</h3>
    <p>
      Mattress protectors may contain waterproof
      membranes or specialist coatings. Use
      appropriate washing temperatures and
      drying methods to protect these materials.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="stain-removal" class="content-section">
    <h2>15. Understanding Stain Removal</h2>

    <p>
      Successful stain removal depends on identifying
      the substance, the fabric, and the appropriate
      treatment. Always check garment-care labels
      and test suitable products before applying
      them to a visible area.
    </p>

    <h3>Protein Stains</h3>
    <p>
      Blood, egg, milk, and some food stains contain
      proteins. Avoid starting with hot water,
      which can set some protein stains. Use an
      appropriate stain treatment and follow
      its directions.
    </p>

    <h3>Oil and Grease</h3>
    <p>
      Cooking oil, butter, cosmetics, and oily
      products can leave greasy marks. A suitable
      liquid laundry detergent or specialist
      grease pre-treatment may help on washable
      fabrics.
    </p>

    <h3>Grass Stains</h3>
    <p>
      Grass stains contain plant pigments and
      other organic material. Treat with a
      compatible stain remover or enzyme
      detergent where suitable for the fabric.
    </p>

    <h3>Wine and Fruit Stains</h3>
    <p>
      Wine, berries, and fruit can leave coloured
      stains. Blot excess liquid gently and use
      an appropriate treatment for the garment.
      Avoid rubbing delicate fabrics.
    </p>

    <h3>Ink Stains</h3>
    <p>
      Ink formulations vary considerably. Identify
      the type of ink where possible and use a
      stain treatment approved for the fabric.
      Some solvents can damage dyes and finishes.
    </p>

    <h3>Makeup and Cosmetic Stains</h3>
    <p>
      Foundation, lipstick, mascara, and other
      cosmetics may contain oils, pigments,
      and waxes. Pretreat only with products
      suitable for the garment and avoid
      spreading the stain.
    </p>

    <h3>General Stain-Removal Principles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Act promptly where possible.</li>
      <li>Blot rather than aggressively rub.</li>
      <li>Identify the fabric before selecting a product.</li>
      <li>Follow the stain remover's instructions.</li>
      <li>Test colourfastness where appropriate.</li>
      <li>Check the stain before tumble drying or ironing.</li>
      <li>Seek professional care for valuable or delicate garments.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="ironing-pressing" class="content-section">
    <h2>16. Ironing, Steaming & Garment Finishing</h2>

    <p>
      Proper finishing can make a significant difference
      to the appearance of clothing. Different fibres
      require different heat settings and techniques.
    </p>

    <h3>Iron Temperature</h3>
    <p>
      Always check the garment label and iron settings.
      Use lower temperatures for delicate synthetic
      fabrics and silk, and higher settings only
      where permitted for suitable cotton and linen.
    </p>

    <h3>Steam Ironing</h3>
    <p>
      Steam can help relax wrinkles in many fabrics.
      Use the manufacturer's recommended water and
      steam settings. Avoid applying steam directly
      to fabrics that may water spot or be damaged
      by moisture.
    </p>

    <h3>Garment Steamers</h3>
    <p>
      Garment steamers are useful for refreshing
      many hanging garments and reducing wrinkles.
      Maintain a safe distance where required
      and check fabric compatibility.
    </p>

    <h3>Pressing Delicate Fabrics</h3>
    <p>
      Use a clean pressing cloth when recommended.
      Avoid excessive pressure and never leave
      a hot iron resting on a garment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="drying-methods" class="content-section">
    <h2>17. Drying & Protecting Garments</h2>

    <h3>Air Drying</h3>
    <p>
      Air drying can help reduce heat-related wear
      and energy use. Choose a well-ventilated
      location and avoid prolonged direct sunlight
      on garments that may fade.
    </p>

    <h3>Flat Drying</h3>
    <p>
      Wool, cashmere, and many delicate knits
      should be reshaped and dried flat when
      instructed by their care labels. This
      helps prevent stretching.
    </p>

    <h3>Tumble Drying</h3>
    <p>
      Use the appropriate dryer setting for
      the fabric. Avoid excessive heat and
      remove suitable garments promptly to
      reduce unnecessary creasing.
    </p>

    <h3>Drying Delicate Garments</h3>
    <p>
      Silk, fine wool, cashmere, and delicate
      garments often require air drying or
      specialized care. Do not assume a
      garment is suitable for the dryer
      simply because it has been machine washed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="laundry-storage" class="content-section">
    <h2>18. Clothing Storage & Preservation</h2>

    <p>
      Good storage helps protect clothing from
      dust, moisture, fading, insects, and
      unnecessary creasing.
    </p>

    <h3>Everyday Clothing</h3>
    <p>
      Ensure garments are clean and completely
      dry before putting them away. Use suitable
      hangers for structured garments and fold
      knitwear to help preserve its shape.
    </p>

    <h3>Fine Garments</h3>
    <p>
      Store silk, cashmere, and other delicate
      clothing in a clean, dry environment.
      Avoid overcrowding closets and protect
      garments from direct sunlight.
    </p>

    <h3>Seasonal Storage</h3>
    <p>
      Before storing winter coats, wool sweaters,
      or summer clothing, clean them according
      to their care labels. Use breathable
      protective storage where appropriate
      and inspect garments periodically.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="laundry-equipment" class="content-section">
    <h2>19. Washing Machines & Laundry Equipment</h2>

    <h3>Washing Machine Cycles</h3>
    <p>
      Modern washing machines offer a variety
      of cycles, including normal, delicate,
      wool, quick wash, heavy duty, and
      bedding programs. Select the cycle
      appropriate for the load and garment
      care instructions.
    </p>

    <h3>Washer Maintenance</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Clean the detergent dispenser regularly.</li>
      <li>Inspect and clean the door seal where applicable.</li>
      <li>Clean the filter according to the manufacturer's instructions.</li>
      <li>Use the recommended machine-cleaning cycle.</li>
      <li>Leave the door open when appropriate to allow the drum to dry.</li>
      <li>Use the correct detergent and recommended dosage.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Dryer Maintenance</h3>
    <p>
      Clean the lint filter after every load and
      follow the manufacturer's instructions for
      maintaining vents and other accessible
      components. Proper maintenance helps
      support safe and efficient operation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="laundry-mistakes" class="content-section">
    <h2>20. Common Laundry Mistakes to Avoid</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Using too much detergent.</li>
      <li>Overloading the washing machine.</li>
      <li>Ignoring garment-care labels.</li>
      <li>Washing delicate fabrics with heavy garments.</li>
      <li>Using unsuitable bleach on coloured or delicate fabrics.</li>
      <li>Applying fabric softener to absorbent towels or technical fabrics.</li>
      <li>Using excessive heat when washing, drying, or ironing.</li>
      <li>Leaving damp laundry sitting in the washing machine.</li>
      <li>Putting stained garments into a hot dryer before checking the stain.</li>
      <li>Storing clothing before it is completely dry.</li>
      <li>Mixing household cleaning chemicals.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      A little preparation and an understanding of fabric
      requirements can help prevent many common laundry
      problems and extend the life of clothing.
    </p>
  </section>

